{
  "analysis": "Round 1 showed small scale (0.3-0.7) is the dominant lever: patches jumped 0.713->0.791, but inclusion stayed flat (0.355) and scratches dropped 0.714->0.667. Miss analysis of the kept model: inclusion misses are overwhelmingly tiny (median 33.8px vs 90.5px train; 146/179 in low tertile, recall 0.178) - the 0.3-0.7 pastes (~27-63px) are still larger than the missed median, so inclusion may need even smaller pastes (0.3-0.5 -> ~27-45px). Inclusion may also be data-starved (10 real images) - round 1's inclusion-heavy test was confounded by default scale, so retest allocation at the known-good small scale. Scratches: the 0.047 AP drop vs control exceeds the noise band, suggesting small pastes of thin lines hurt; missed scratches (median 41.8px) sit between the two tested ranges, so probe a mid scale 0.5-1.0. Three one-factor tests vs the kept plan: (1) inclusion scale 0.3-0.5, (2) inclusion-heavy allocation 24/13/13, (3) scratch scale 0.5-1.0. All keep same_class pool (any_class hurt inclusion badly in R1).",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "Inclusion misses center on ~34px instances, smaller than the 0.3-0.7 pastes produced in R1 (which left inclusion AP flat at 0.355). Shrinking inclusion pastes to 0.3-0.5 (~27-45px from ~90px sources) should match the missed size band and lift inclusion AP50 above ~0.40 with improved low-tertile recall; if inclusion AP drops instead, 0.3-0.7 was already the sweet spot and scale is not the inclusion lever.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 17, "patches": 17, "scratches": 16},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.5, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Inclusion is data-starved (10 real images, AP 0.355, recall 0.25) and R1's inclusion-heavy test was confounded by using default scale. Shifting budget to inclusion (24/13/13) while keeping the proven 0.3-0.7 small scale should raise inclusion AP50 above ~0.40 without dropping patches/scratches by more than ~0.03; if inclusion stays ~0.35, allocation is not the lever and the problem is purely scale/appearance.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 24, "patches": 13, "scratches": 13},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Scratch AP fell 0.714->0.667 when pastes shrank to 0.3-0.7 (beyond the ~0.03 noise band), suggesting very small thin-line pastes are unlearnable noise, yet missed scratches (median 41.8px) are small. A mid scale 0.5-1.0 (~36-73px from ~73px sources) should recover scratch AP50 toward ~0.71 while keeping patches/inclusion at their proven 0.3-0.7; if scratches do not recover, the R1 drop was noise and per-class scale tuning is unnecessary.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 17, "patches": 17, "scratches": 16},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.5, "scale_max": 1.0, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Candidate 2 (inclusion-heavy 24/13/13 at 0.3-0.7) hit the best mAP 0.625 and best worst-class AP 0.3845, with inclusion recall jumping 0.307->0.363 — confirming inclusion is data-limited, not scale-limited. Candidate 1 (inclusion 0.3-0.5) was nearly tied (0.6205) but with a lower worst-class AP (0.3786), so shrinking pastes smaller did not clearly beat 0.3-0.7. Candidate 3 (scratch 0.5-1.0) was clearly worse (mAP 0.593, scratch AP 0.657 did not recover), refuting the 'small pastes hurt scratches' hypothesis. The control (repeat of R1) gave 0.591 with large per-class swings (patches 0.79->0.71, inclusion 0.35->0.41), confirming ~0.05-0.08 single-run noise. Candidates 2 and 1 are within 0.005 mAP of the best, so the tie-break (higher worst-class AP50) selects candidate 2.",
  "choice": 2,
  "lesson": "Inclusion-heavy allocation (24/13/13) at small scale 0.3-0.7 gave the best mAP (0.625) and best worst-class AP (0.3845) with inclusion recall up to 0.363 — inclusion is data-limited, so keep feeding it. Scratch mid-scale 0.5-1.0 was clearly worse (mAP 0.593, scratch AP did not recover), refuting 'small pastes hurt scratches' — keep scratches at 0.3-0.7. The control (repeat R1) swung wildly (patches 0.79->0.71, inclusion 0.35->0.41) at mAP 0.591, so per-class deltas under ~0.05 are noise; in the final round build on inclusion-heavy + 0.3-0.7 and avoid untested extreme allocations."
}
